virglrenderer: Venus copies, blits and resolves stay inside their images and buffers

What: each region of vkCmdCopyImage(2), vkCmdBlitImage(2),
vkCmdResolveImage(2), vkCmdCopyBufferToImage(2) and vkCmdCopyImageToBuffer(2)
names an aspect the image's format has (a plane of a multi-planar format,
depth and/or stencil, else color), a non-empty extent and texels inside the
level (a plane's share of it; block formats in whole blocks; between formats
of other block sizes the destination's extent in its own blocks); blit
corners lie inside the levels; a buffer-image copy's row length and image
height are 0 or at least the extent, and the bytes from its buffer offset to
the end of its last texel block (overflow-checked) lie inside the buffer.
The format facts come from src/venus/vkr_format_extent.h, generated from the
Vulkan registry like vkr_format_table.h. A refused command ends the app's
context.
Why: both drivers record such regions; the GPU would then read or write
outside the image or buffer when the commands run (not run here: that needs
a submit, and a GPU fault once ended in a macOS panic).
virgl-venus-command-limits.patch checks the levels and layers only.
Upstream: OmacVM policy (spec VUIDs), draft only.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-commands.c (the c-region- cases);
Tests/virgl/venus-format-extent.py writes the header.

